<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco de Almeida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1510">1510</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco de Almeida was a Portuguese nobleman and the first viceroy of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He established fortified bases on the East African and Indian coasts and defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Gujarati fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> in 1509, securing Portuguese naval dominance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida.jpg" alt="Francisco de Almeida" width="225" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_D._Francisco_de_Almeida_(ap%C3%B3s_1545)_-_Autor_desconhecido.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 24 July 1505 <b>Francisco de Almeida</b> landed about 500 men in two columns; the defenders fought from the rooftops with arrows and stones, but the Emir escaped to the mainland and the town was sacked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Overseas power rested on ships and guns: caravels and carracks armed with artillery let small fleets dominate the Indian Ocean after <b>Francisco de Almeida</b>’s victory at the Battle of Diu in 1509, and Afonso de Albuquerque seized Goa in 1510, Malacca in 1511 and Hormuz in 1515.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In August 1505 <b>Francisco de Almeida</b>, on his way to become viceroy of Portuguese India, stormed Mombasa after fierce resistance, sacked and burned it and carried off a large booty; the ruler fled to the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Building on the Estado da Índia created under the first viceroy, <b>Francisco de Almeida</b>, in 1505, Afonso de Albuquerque, governor of Portuguese India from 1509 to 1515, turned da Gama’s commercial opening into a strategic empire by seizing Goa in 1510, Malacca in 1511 and Hormuz in 1515,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1510, at the Battle of Salt River, ox-mounted ǃUriǁ’aekua, the Goringhaiqua of Dutch records, defeated the party of <b>Francisco de Almeida</b>, killing him and fifty of his men.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1505 a Portuguese fleet under <b>Francisco de Almeida</b> sacked Kilwa and Mombasa.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1489" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 775 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco de Almeida"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1489">What was here in 1489? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
